  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A little bit cooler tomorrow inland, but \ngenerally remaining near to above normal through the upcoming \nweekend. Uncertainty remains for the potential for some rain \nthis weekend. \n\nLatest weather analysis reveals persistent upper level ridging \nover the central Gulf coast, extending north into southeast \nCanada. Meanwhile, another upper trough continues to dig across \nthe PAC NW into the northern Rockies. At the surface, a weak/dry\ncold front has pushed through the area this afternoon, with \n1034+ mb surface high pressure lingering over Ontario and \nQuebec. Temperatures largely in the low to mid 70s across inland\nareas, with the onshore flow bringing a slightly cooler but \nstill seasonable afternoon along the coast, with readings well \ninto the 60s to near 70.\n\nThe high behind the front slide offshore of coastal New England\nlate tonight and will ridge south into the mid-Atlantic region.\nResultant light onshore flow will result in developing low to\nmid clouds late tonight into Wednesday morning, which will bring\nsome patchy fog mainly along and west of I-95 within a few hours\nof sunrise Wed morning. The low clouds linger for a good part of\nthe morning inland, and have accordingly knocked temperatures\ndown a few degrees west of I-95. Meanwhile, along the coast and\nover much of the Delmarva, a good amount of sunshine will bring  \nanother seasonably pleasant day with highs right around 70\ndegrees. \n\nThe high will then eventually anchor off the southern New \nEngland to Mid- Atlantic coast Wednesday night and Thursday, \nwhile low pressure remains suppressed well south near the \nBahamas. With the high pressure offshore, building SSW flow \nallows for a warmer end to the week, with highs to potentially \nreach the the middle to upper 70s Thursday and Friday, before\ncooling slightly with increasing clouds on Halloween.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Remaining dry this week. Uncertainty remains \nfor the potential for some rain late this weekend into early\nnext week.\n\nDry weather conditions are forecasted to prevail through the \nlate weekend, as upper ridging builds over the region. By Friday\nand into the weekend, another northern stream shortwave \ntraversing central Canada will flatten the upper pattern into \nthe weekend. Halloween continues to look dry pleasant, and mild \nwith increasing clouds ahead of of a weak cold front allowing \nfor optimal conditions for Trick-or-Treaters Sat night. Models \ncontinue to slow the approach of the lead shortwave pushing into\nthe region Sunday into early next week. There remains the \npotential for the pattern to become unsettled for next week. The\nlatest 12z/27 ensembles continue to hint on a weak system \nmoving across the area during the first couple days of November,\nwith several other weak systems on its heels for later next \nweek. Still some typical jostling among the probabilistic models\nregarding rain chances, with the EPS/CMC ensemble a bit more \nbullish on QPF than the GFS. So while some welcome rain does \nappear on the horizon for next week, none of the available \nguidance showing prospects for any drought-busting rains.",
